WEBVTT

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:04.710
New England contains different rock belts, faults, folds, and metamorphic rocks.

00:00:04.890 --> 00:00:08.170
The supplied map and cross section show structures at the surface

00:00:08.170 --> 00:00:09.970
and interpreted relations below it.

00:00:10.150 --> 00:00:13.130
Use the legend and orientation before comparing locations.

00:00:13.570 --> 00:00:17.790
Paleozoic collisions contributed to Appalachian deformation and metamorphism.

00:00:18.050 --> 00:00:22.410
Later Mesozoic extension opened rift basins as North America separated from

00:00:22.410 --> 00:00:24.390
Africa. These are separate events;

00:00:24.510 --> 00:00:27.230
a modern map can display structures inherited from both.

00:00:27.770 --> 00:00:31.030
The source map shows the highest metamorphic conditions in the central

00:00:31.030 --> 00:00:35.050
part of its illustrated area, decreasing toward both east and west.

00:00:35.230 --> 00:00:39.730
Grade is constrained using mineral assemblages and textures, not topographic elevation

00:00:39.730 --> 00:00:40.270
alone.

00:00:40.970 --> 00:00:44.390
In the supplied regional interpretation, rocks in eastern Vermont were buried

00:00:44.390 --> 00:00:48.030
and then heated, while rocks in New Hampshire were initially heated

00:00:48.030 --> 00:00:53.310
and then buried. These paths are evidence-based reconstructions for specific locations,

00:00:53.310 --> 00:00:56.250
not a universal order for all mountain belts.
